-- title:
--   Constructibly closed sets in spectral spaces: specializations, closedness criterion, and total separatedness of T1 spectral spaces
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a spectral space. Its constructible topology is generated by the quasi-compact open subsets and their complements.
--
--   Principal results:
--   1. (Stacks 0903(1)) Let $s \subseteq X$ be closed in the constructible topology and $x \in \overline{s}$, the closure in the original topology. Then there is $y \in s$ that specializes to $x$, that is, $x \in \overline{\{y\}}$.
--   2. (Stacks 0903(2)) If $s \subseteq X$ is closed in the constructible topology and stable under specialization, then $s$ is closed in $X$.
--   3. (Stacks 0905, (6)⇒(1)) A spectral space that is $T_1$ is totally separated: any two distinct points are separated by a clopen set. In fact, every quasi-compact open is then clopen.
--
--   These results are used for w-local spaces, where the subspace of closed points of a spectral space must be shown to be profinite.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The declarations are `exists_specializes_of_isClosed_constructibleTopology_of_mem_closure`, `IsClosed.of_isClosed_constructibleTopology` and the instance `SpectralSpace.totallySeparatedSpace` (assuming `[SpectralSpace X] [T1Space X]`). Constructible closedness is written `IsClosed[constructibleTopology X] s`, using Mathlib's constructible topology. "$y$ specializes to $x$" is `y ⤳ x`.
-- source:
--   B. Bhatt, P. Scholze, The pro-étale topology for schemes, https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.1198 (v2, 17 Dec 2014); cited in the code: Stacks Project, Tag 0903; Stacks Project, Tag 0905. Lean source: idealson-nexources/proetale (fork of chrisflav/proetale by C. Merten, J. Jiang and contributors, Apache-2.0), commit 2983805, file Proetale/Topology/SpectralSpace/Constructible.lean (declarations needed by the transplanted proofs, unused ones removed).

